     r=4 reaches 0         -> empty, so a generic determinantal quartic
     surface in P^3 is smooth and the discriminant does NOT vanish on D_4.

     deg disc(Sym^4 C^r) = r . 3^{r-1} : {4: 108, 5: 405, 6: 1458, 7: 5103}

supporting:  rank Hess(det_4)(M) as a function of rank M
     rank M   rank Hess (Q)   mod p1   mod p2   2n = 8?
          4              16       16       16   no      (12 draws, all equal)
          3               8        8        8   yes      (12 draws, all equal)
          2               4        4        4   yes      (12 draws, all equal)
          1               0        0        0   yes      (12 draws, all equal)
          0               0        0        0   yes      (12 draws, all equal)

     rank <= 3 (i.e. every point of the hypersurface) gives rank Hess <= 8,
     so the LMR rank condition holds at EVERY point of {det A(s) = 0},
     not only at generic ones -- which is what the divisibility needs.

     This is a PROOF, not a sample.  Hess(det_4)(X M Y) = (X (x) Y)^T .
     Hess(det_4)(M) . (X (x) Y) up to the scalar det X det Y, so rank Hess
     is constant on GL_4 x GL_4 orbits, and each rank stratum of M_4 is a
     single such orbit.  The draws above therefore determine the value on
     the whole stratum; the assertion that every draw in a stratum agrees
     is the check on that.
